# Line endings: the repository stores LF for every text file, and every OS checks text out
# with LF - also with core.autocrlf=true (an eol attribute wins over it). Only Windows batch
# files check out with CRLF (cmd.exe misreads labels / goto in LF files). The working tree
# already matches this, so a fresh commit leaves `git status` clean.
# tests/test_round8_r.py checks these rules and the bytes of the files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, so git never has to guess (shell scripts and the Dockerfile break with CRLF)
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf

# Windows batch files
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.tgz binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
